{
  "teamId": 112,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Chicago Cubs entered the season with genuine World Series aspirations, the core of their 2016 championship still largely intact. But the year would become a story of false hope, dissolution, and ultimately, a painful rebuild that nobody wanted to admit was coming. April's 11-15 record masked a roster already showing its age, even as individual heroes — Willson Contreras launching clutch home runs, a Dan Winkler double-play gem saving a win — kept the team afloat in the early going.\n\nMay was the great mirage. The Cubs went 19-8, their best month of the season, powered by Javier Báez at his electric best and timely hitting up and down the lineup. Back-to-back walk-off moments against the Dodgers, Ian Happ's three-run blast and then the Rizzo-Duffy combo, made it feel like the old Cubs magic was alive. But the front office was already reading a different ledger, and as June cooled to 12-16 and July limped in at 9-16, the trade deadline loomed like a guillotine. Kris Bryant, Anthony Rizzo, Javier Báez, and others were shipped out in what became one of baseball's most stunning single-deadline teardowns.\n\nAugust was the crater — a brutal 7-20 record as a roster stripped to its bones tried to find its footing. Yet even in the wreckage, stories emerged. Frank Schwindel arrived from nowhere to become a legitimate fan favorite, slashing hits and doubles in the cleanup spot. Ian Happ's three-run homer on August 25th and a wild Trevor Story error that gifted the Cubs a run showed the team still had fight. And then September brought the most unexpected character of all: Schwindel's grand slam on September 5th was the kind of moment that made Cubs fans briefly forget what had just been dismantled.\n\nThe final weeks were a quiet postscript to a season that had already said its goodbyes. Trayce Thompson homered twice in the last two games — including a grand slam on October 2nd — while Ian Happ closed the curtain with the season's single highest-leverage swing, a two-run blast that was equal parts defiant and elegiac. The Cubs finished 71-91, their worst record in years, but the season's final image was a team of castoffs swinging hard until the last out, planting a flag for whoever would carry this thing forward.",
